1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,504
How do you know the person signing up really owns that email address?

2
00:00:03,554 --> 00:00:07,128
Anyone can type anyone's email into a sign-up form.

3
00:00:07,178 --> 00:00:10,848
Picture a stranger who signs up a shop with a famous brand's address.

4
00:00:10,898 --> 00:00:18,168
Later the bank sends contracts, reports and payout notices to an inbox the real owner never controlled.

5
00:00:18,218 --> 00:00:20,904
So the bank does what a postcard with a code does.

6
00:00:20,954 --> 00:00:27,480
Some services mail a postcard to an address, and only someone who gets that post can read the code and type it back.

7
00:00:27,530 --> 00:00:29,352
Here is how it works.

8
00:00:29,402 --> 00:00:31,584
A person fills in the sign-up form.

9
00:00:31,634 --> 00:00:36,816
Their password goes straight to the login server, the one place that checks passwords.

10
00:00:36,866 --> 00:00:40,056
It never touches ecom's own database.

11
00:00:40,106 --> 00:00:43,920
ecom saves the new merchant as pending, and nothing more.

12
00:00:43,970 --> 00:00:47,664
Then ecom sends a one-time code to the email address.

13
00:00:47,714 --> 00:00:51,912
A one-time code is a short number that works once, for a short time.

14
00:00:51,962 --> 00:00:54,744
Only someone who can read that inbox can type it back.

15
00:00:54,794 --> 00:00:58,944
The store that keeps the codes never keeps the code itself, or the email.

16
00:00:58,994 --> 00:01:04,800
It keeps only fingerprints of them: enough to check a match, useless to anyone who steals them.

17
00:01:04,850 --> 00:01:11,352
When the right code comes back, that is email verification: proof that the person controls the address.

18
00:01:11,402 --> 00:01:13,224
Wrong guesses are counted.

19
00:01:13,274 --> 00:01:19,872
Too many wrong codes, and the sign-up is locked until the window ends, even if a new code is asked for.

20
00:01:19,922 --> 00:01:21,744
That is a lockout.

21
00:01:21,794 --> 00:01:24,432
Every refusal says when to try again.

22
00:01:24,482 --> 00:01:29,256
And if the store that keeps the codes is down, nothing is sent and nothing is accepted.

23
00:01:29,306 --> 00:01:31,896
The system fails closed, never open.

24
00:01:31,946 --> 00:01:33,768
Now watch the product.

25
00:01:33,818 --> 00:01:37,056
The viewer opens the merchant portal and clicks to sign up.

26
00:01:37,106 --> 00:01:47,472
The page says "Create a merchant account", and under it, "We will email you a code to confirm the address." There are three boxes: company name, work email and password.

27
00:01:47,522 --> 00:01:50,040
The viewer fills them in and clicks Create account.

28
00:01:50,090 --> 00:01:51,912
Now the test inbox.

29
00:01:51,962 --> 00:01:55,536
A new email has arrived, with a six-digit code inside.

30
00:01:55,586 --> 00:02:01,704
Back in the portal, the page says "Check your email." It asks for the code, and says it works for a few minutes.

31
00:02:01,754 --> 00:02:05,832
While the code box is not full, the Verify button stays grey.

32
00:02:05,882 --> 00:02:12,576
The button under it, "Send a new code", counts down the seconds before it can be pressed again, so nobody can flood an inbox.

33
00:02:12,626 --> 00:02:16,560
The viewer types the six digits, and the Verify button lights up.

34
00:02:16,610 --> 00:02:18,432
One click.

35
00:02:18,482 --> 00:02:22,464
The page says "Email verified", and tells the person to sign in.

36
00:02:22,514 --> 00:02:25,680
It also says the bank will now check their documents.

37
00:02:25,730 --> 00:02:32,952
Behind that screen, the person who signed up becomes the merchant's first admin, both in ecom and on the login server.

38
00:02:33,002 --> 00:02:34,824
What does this protect?

39
00:02:34,874 --> 00:02:38,736
Without it, anyone could open a merchant account in someone else's name.

40
00:02:38,786 --> 00:02:44,448
Contracts, reports and payout notices would go to an address the real owner never saw.

41
00:02:44,498 --> 00:02:47,880
The one-time code protects the real owner of the address.

42
00:02:47,930 --> 00:02:53,088
It protects the bank too, because every later message reaches the person who really asked.

43
00:02:53,138 --> 00:02:56,184
The lockout stops a stranger from guessing the code.

44
00:02:56,234 --> 00:03:01,296
And failing closed means a broken code store can never let an unchecked sign-up through.

45
00:03:01,346 --> 00:03:08,016
So, like the postcard with a code: only the inbox's owner can answer, and only the inbox's owner gets in.

46
00:03:08,066 --> 00:03:11,496
Next: how a merchant earns the right to take money.
